{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Sarai his daughter-in-law, the wife of Abram his son.</b> It was necessary to say 'the wife of Abram' even though he had already mentioned 'his daughter-in-law,' because he is called Abram on account of being a father to Ram — meaning Aram — or Abraham on account of 'a father of a multitude of nations' (Genesis 17:5), and she too is called Sarai on account of dominion. And in the matter of dominion, Abraham is certainly the principal and Sarah is subordinate to him; therefore it says 'Sarai the wife of Abram' — just as every wife is subordinate to her husband, so in the matter of dominion she is subordinate to him. But in the matter of the holy spirit, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 1:1) that Abraham was subordinate to Sarah in prophecy. Therefore, when he called her by the name 'Yiskah,' which indicates that she was overshadowed by the holy spirit upon her, he did not mention there 'the wife of Abram.' Even though Rashi explained 'Yiskah' as a term of dominion, from the language of 'princes of men' (Michah 5:4), nevertheless 'overshadowed by the holy spirit' is also implied."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:51.189683723Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}